          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes reassessing blood pressure about once a month after treatment starts or changes, until the target is reached. Once readings are stable, review every three to six months. The guidance also says that after starting a medicine that acts on the renin-angiotensin system, a check of kidney function and potassium is part of that monthly review. (Source: \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line,\" and \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets,\" both from a major cardiology society consensus summary.)\n\nThe passages do not say how long to wait before the first check, and they do not cover any specific medicine. Some guidance differs on when to start treatment at all. Your own clinician can tell you what schedule fits your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
